REV.page({"course":"english1","id":"w01","title":"Learning language and the sounds of English","summary":"How grown-ups learn a new language, why English letters and English sounds are two separate systems, and the 44 sounds of English (20 vowels and 24 consonants), with where and how each sound is made, the diphthongs and semi-vowels, and the rules for consonant clusters.","n":1,"lectures":[{"code":"1.1","title":"Introduction - english course | building fluency, accuracy & confidence in communication","video":"EPa6ATqMI9g","seconds":391},{"code":"1.2","title":"Learning language | acquisition, patterns & effective strategies for english mastery","video":"DgKYu1pi3qg","seconds":1306},{"code":"1.3","title":"Sounds & writing symbols in english","video":"xcc0U_U4xhg","seconds":2043},{"code":"1.4","title":"Speech sounds in english","video":"MMBrfQC0V9A","seconds":1982},{"code":"1.5","title":"Articulation of vowel & consonant sounds in english","video":"fC-UbX3MsGs","seconds":1638},{"code":"1.6","title":"Diphthongs & semi-vowels","video":"WqiKRiJTqnc","seconds":1792},{"code":"1.7","title":"Consonant clusters in english words","video":"Pj1iWVqBuyg","seconds":1974}],"toc":[{"id":"at-a-glance","level":2,"title":"At a glance"},{"id":"the-course-accuracy-fluency-confidence","level":2,"title":"The course: accuracy, fluency, confidence"},{"id":"how-we-learn-a-language","level":2,"title":"How we learn a language"},{"id":"letters-and-sounds","level":2,"title":"Letters and sounds"},{"id":"26-letters-two-sets","level":3,"title":"26 letters, two sets"},{"id":"a-for-apple","level":3,"title":"A for apple"},{"id":"writing-a-sound","level":3,"title":"Writing a sound"},{"id":"the-44-sounds-of-english","level":3,"title":"The 44 sounds of English"},{"id":"how-speech-sounds-are-made","level":2,"title":"How speech sounds are made"},{"id":"vowels-and-consonants","level":3,"title":"Vowels and consonants"},{"id":"the-articulators","level":3,"title":"The articulators"},{"id":"the-english-consonant-chart","level":3,"title":"The English consonant chart"},{"id":"aspiration-voicing-and-indian-english","level":2,"title":"Aspiration, voicing and Indian English"},{"id":"describing-vowels","level":3,"title":"Describing vowels"},{"id":"aspiration-and-voicing","level":3,"title":"Aspiration and voicing"},{"id":"english-has-no-retroflex-sounds","level":3,"title":"English has no retroflex sounds"},{"id":"diphthongs","level":2,"title":"Diphthongs"},{"id":"semi-vowels","level":2,"title":"Semi-vowels"},{"id":"sound-not-spelling","level":3,"title":"Sound, not spelling"},{"id":"never-at-the-end-of-a-word","level":3,"title":"Never at the end of a word"},{"id":"their-function-joining-two-vowels","level":3,"title":"Their function: joining two vowels"},{"id":"consonant-clusters","level":2,"title":"Consonant clusters"},{"id":"words-as-patterns-of-c-and-v","level":3,"title":"Words as patterns of C and V"},{"id":"what-a-cluster-is","level":3,"title":"What a cluster is"},{"id":"manner-and-the-same-place-rule","level":3,"title":"Manner and the same-place rule"},{"id":"clusters-anywhere-but-not-in-random-order","level":3,"title":"Clusters anywhere, but not in random order"},{"id":"clusters-of-three-consonants","level":3,"title":"Clusters of three consonants"},{"id":"summary","level":2,"title":"Summary"},{"id":"check-yourself","level":2,"title":"Check yourself"}],"html":"<h2 id=\"at-a-glance\">At a glance</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The goal</strong> (1.1): accuracy leads to fluency, and the two together give <strong>confidence</strong>. The course aims at <em>communicative confidence</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Acquisition and learning</strong> (1.2): children <strong>acquire</strong> their first language automatically (before about 10 years of age). Grown-ups <strong>learn</strong> a new one with effort (after about 15), by observing patterns and applying them. Listen to English and read English for at least 30 minutes a day each.</li>\n<li><strong>Letters are not sounds</strong> (1.3): English writes with <strong>26 letters</strong> but speaks with <strong>44 sounds: 20 vowels and 24 consonants</strong>. <em>Cat</em> begins with the letter c but with the sound /k/.</li>\n<li><strong>How sounds are made</strong> (1.4): all speech uses the air we breathe <strong>out</strong>. Vowels have almost no obstruction; consonants are blocked or narrowed at a <strong>place of articulation</strong> (lips, teeth, alveolar ridge, palate, velum, glottis). Air through the mouth only gives an <strong>oral</strong> sound; air also through the nose gives a <strong>nasal</strong> sound.</li>\n<li><strong>Aspiration and voicing</strong> (1.5): a puff of air and a vibration of the vocal cords tell k, kh, g and gh apart. English has <strong>no retroflex sounds</strong>: English t and d are <strong>alveolar</strong>, and English f is not the ph of our languages.</li>\n<li><strong>Diphthongs</strong> (1.6): 8 of the 20 vowels glide from one vowel quality to another (/eɪ/ in <em>take</em>). The other 12 are <strong>monophthongs</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Semi-vowels</strong> (1.6): /w/ and /j/ (the y sound) are consonants made almost like vowels. They never end a word, and in speech they join a vowel at the end of one word to a vowel at the start of the next (<em>go-w-away</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Consonant clusters</strong> (1.7): consonants together with no vowel between them (<em>gl</em> in <em>glass</em>). Two non-nasal sounds from the same place never cluster (<em>pb</em>), and a three-consonant cluster at the start of a word is always <strong>s + p, t or k + r or l</strong> (<em>spring, stress, splash</em>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-course-accuracy-fluency-confidence\">The course: accuracy, fluency, confidence</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 1.1 is the welcome. The teacher, Professor Rajesh Kumar of the Department of Humanities and Social Sciences, IIT Madras, teaches <strong>linguistics</strong>, the scientific study of how language is structured. He studies the processes under sentence structure and uses them for teaching language. The course has one clear goal: <strong>communicative confidence</strong>, the confidence to speak English fluently and to communicate well with others.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Accuracy, fluency and confidence</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecture asks us to notice three terms, “in that order” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EPa6ATqMI9g&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Accuracy</strong>: using the language correctly.</li>\n<li><strong>Fluency</strong>: speaking (or reading) smoothly, without stopping. The slide’s picture says: “I can read accurately with expression &amp; understand what I read.”</li>\n<li><strong>Confidence</strong>: accuracy leads to fluency, and the <strong>combination of the two</strong> gives confidence. That confidence builds <strong>communicative confidence</strong>, and with it we communicate effectively.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>Accuracy → fluency → accuracy + fluency → confidence → communicative confidence.</strong></p>\n<p>Why English? It is one of the most widely spoken languages in the world (the slides show a map of the English-speaking countries and of the percentage of English speakers). We speak many languages in India, but with people who do not speak our languages we usually use English (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EPa6ATqMI9g&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>). People speak English differently all over the world, and we in India speak it in our own way. The course will show what makes “our English” ours, how it differs from other varieties, and how to improve it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EPa6ATqMI9g&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>).</p>\n<p>Effective communication brings <strong>success</strong> in every domain of life. The course works on it through speaking, writing and reading (“just to name a few”). You will learn to see the <strong>underlying patterns</strong> of the language and to apply them: these skills come from how language is computed in the subconscious of the human mind (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EPa6ATqMI9g&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>). (<em>Subconscious</em> = the part of the mind that works without our noticing.)</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Multilingual method</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“The way we speak English and the way we communicate with others in a normal and natural situation is called <strong>multilingual method</strong>” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EPa6ATqMI9g&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>). The course uses it in the background. It builds not just structure but <strong>confidence</strong>, “the most required tool for learning any new language”, and it develops <strong>cognitive flexibility</strong>: a flexible way of thinking (<em>cognitive</em> = to do with thinking) that helps in speaking, writing and reading.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"how-we-learn-a-language\">How we learn a language</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 1.2. Language is “one of the most sophisticated products of the human mind”. The mind is a complex thing, but it <strong>likes simplicity and patterns</strong>. So learning a language means finding the <strong>patterns</strong> under it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DgKYu1pi3qg&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>). The lecturer links this to our degree: this course is part of a programme in data science, and understanding how language works also helps in understanding how data works (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DgKYu1pi3qg&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>).</p>\n<p>The key habit for the whole course: <strong>pay attention</strong>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Every normal human child is born with the capacity to learn language. At three or four years of age children start speaking <strong>words</strong>, then <strong>sequences of two words</strong>, then <strong>small phrases</strong>, and soon <strong>sentences</strong>. Nobody teaches them: it happens automatically (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DgKYu1pi3qg&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Acquisition and learning</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Acquisition</strong>: getting a language <strong>automatically</strong>, without effort. Children acquire their first language.</li>\n<li><strong>Learning</strong>: getting a language through <strong>effort</strong>. Grown-ups learn a new language.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>(<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DgKYu1pi3qg&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>)</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture draws two lines: before <strong>10</strong> years of age and after <strong>15</strong>. The years in between are a transition; you could also draw one line at 12. “These numbers are not important” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DgKYu1pi3qg&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>).</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Early (before 10)</th>\n<th>Late (after 15)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>How it feels</td>\n<td>automatic and effortless</td>\n<td>requires efforts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>How it happens</td>\n<td>the mind processes what the child hears</td>\n<td>conscious efforts, with the subconscious process</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Starting point</td>\n<td>the language around the child</td>\n<td>another language exists already</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>What helps</td>\n<td>input from the people around</td>\n<td>specific and directed motivations; it is easy to work on structures</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sounds</td>\n<td>these years shape the vocal tract</td>\n<td>not possible to change the conditioning of the vocal tract</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The rows for late learning are the slide’s five points (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DgKYu1pi3qg&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>). (<em>Structures</em> = the patterns of words and sentences.)</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Learning language is child’s play</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide’s statement (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DgKYu1pi3qg&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>): “A normal human child is <strong>hardwired</strong> to acquire language from the immediate environment with the help of <strong>Language Acquisition Device (LAD)</strong> and <strong>Universal Grammar (UG)</strong>.”</p>\n<p><strong>Input</strong> (from the immediate society; fuzzy and limited) → <strong>Human mind</strong> (LAD/UG) → <strong>Output</strong> (grammatical and infinite)</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Hardwired</em> = built in from birth. LAD is the name for this inborn language ability; UG is the name for the basic plan that all human languages share.</li>\n<li><strong>Input</strong>: what the child hears. It is random, <strong>fuzzy</strong> (unclear, broken), very limited, and not designed for teaching.</li>\n<li><strong>Computation</strong>: the mind processes this input. The lecture calls this “computation of language in human mind”.</li>\n<li><strong>Output</strong>: what the child says. It is <strong>systematic, grammatical, meaningful and infinite</strong>: the child can say sentences it has never heard.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>The process stays with grown-ups, but it becomes <strong>weaker</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DgKYu1pi3qg&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>). So after 15 we need <strong>conscious effort</strong>, combined with <strong>specific and directed motivation</strong> (a clear reason to learn). If you have ever learned a language, check: you had such a motivation (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DgKYu1pi3qg&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">How a grown-up learns</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Observe the patterns, try to understand, apply the pattern and leave the subconscious process to function” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DgKYu1pi3qg&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>). There is nothing to <strong>memorise</strong>. Children find patterns without knowing it; grown-ups can <strong>see</strong> patterns consciously, and that is our advantage.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Vocal tract</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The passage the air passes through when we speak: from the lungs up through the throat, the mouth and the nose. It contains the <strong>articulators</strong>, the parts that shape sounds (tongue, lips, teeth and others). Lecture 1.4 shows a picture of it.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>By 15, the <strong>sound system</strong> of the language we learned before 10 has <strong>conditioned</strong> our vocal tract (trained it into fixed habits). This is why we are native speakers of our first language. The conditioning is “so permanent and rigid” that we cannot fully get the sound patterns of a new language (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DgKYu1pi3qg&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>). This must not worry us: by understanding the differences and making conscious efforts we <strong>improve</strong> the way we speak, even if we do not change it completely.</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">You do not have to sound American or British</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“You don’t have to sound like an American or a British or Australian. You have to sound like yourself” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DgKYu1pi3qg&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>). The lecturer calls this “quite scientific”: it follows from how the vocal tract is conditioned. Aim to be clear and impressive, not to copy an accent.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The slide <strong>Learning as subconscious process</strong> lists four skills: <strong>listening, speaking, writing, reading</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DgKYu1pi3qg&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>). “A good speaker must be a good listener”, and the lecturer means it literally.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Listening</strong>: listen to any audio in English for <strong>at least half an hour every day</strong>, with attention to how the speaker speaks. News bulletins are good; anything else also works (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DgKYu1pi3qg&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Reading</strong>: read for <strong>at least half an hour every day</strong>: any literature you like (stories, novels, science fiction, politics) <strong>and</strong> a newspaper every day. Newspapers have “simple, active, affirmative sentences” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DgKYu1pi3qg&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>). (<em>Active</em>: the doer comes first, as in “The minister opened the bridge”. <em>Affirmative</em>: positive, not negative.)</li>\n<li><strong>Speaking</strong>: listening and reading improve your speaking in the subconscious. You do not need a special effort for it; just keep speaking.</li>\n<li><strong>Writing</strong>: a separate, specific skill. Millions of people speak a language but do not write it. The course will work on writing too (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DgKYu1pi3qg&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">The daily routine</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>30 minutes of listening + 30 minutes of reading, every day. They feed your speaking.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"letters-and-sounds\">Letters and sounds</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 1.3 separates two systems that we usually mix up:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the <strong>writing system</strong>: how we <strong>spell</strong> words, with letters;</li>\n<li>the <strong>sound system</strong>: what happens when we <strong>speak</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>They are “very different things” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xcc0U_U4xhg&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>). If this surprises you, the lecturer says, you need even closer attention to it. Children learn to speak intuitively, but writing is a skill learned with effort.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"26-letters-two-sets\">26 letters, two sets</h3>\n<p>English writes with <strong>26 letters</strong>: a b c d e f g h i j k l m n o p q r s t u v w x y z (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xcc0U_U4xhg&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>). The last letter is <strong>zee</strong> in American English and <strong>zed</strong> in British English.</p>\n<p>Each letter has two forms, <strong>uppercase</strong> (capital) and <strong>lowercase</strong> (small). We use a capital at the start of the first word of a sentence, at the start of proper names, and in abbreviations. But the lecturer’s real question is: <em>why</em> two sets, and couldn’t we manage with one? He leaves it for you to think about (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xcc0U_U4xhg&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>).</p>\n<p>He calls the letters <strong>symbols</strong> on purpose: they are marks we use to <strong>represent</strong> the sounds we speak. They are not the sounds.</p>\n<h3 id=\"a-for-apple\">A for apple</h3>\n<p>Children learn the letters through rhymes and then “A for apple, B for ball, C for cat, D for dog, E for elephant, F for fan, G for girl, H for house, I for ink”. The slide stops at I and leaves J to Z blank for you to fill (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xcc0U_U4xhg&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>).</p>\n<p>Now say <em>apple</em> and listen: where is the sound of the letter name A in it? There is none (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xcc0U_U4xhg&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>). “A for apple” teaches the first <strong>letter</strong> of the written word, not its first sound. The same is true for the others:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>cat</em>: the first letter is c, but the first <strong>sound</strong> is /k/ (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xcc0U_U4xhg&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>). Neither the name of c nor anything like it is heard in <em>cat</em>.</li>\n<li><em>elephant</em> does not begin with the name of E, and <em>fan</em> does not begin with the name of F (“ef”).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">No one-to-one correspondence</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“There is no direct <strong>one-to-one correspondence</strong> between the sound system of English, that is the way words are spoken, and the way it is written with the symbols representing those sounds” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xcc0U_U4xhg&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>). <em>One-to-one</em> would mean one letter for each sound and one sound for each letter. English does not work like that, and this makes learning a little harder.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Three steps of learning</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>From the lecture (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xcc0U_U4xhg&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Pay attention</strong> to differences and similarities.</li>\n<li><strong>Allow</strong> your subconscious to fix them.</li>\n<li><strong>Observe your own output</strong>: check whether your speaking shows the learning.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"writing-a-sound\">Writing a sound</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Phonetic transcription</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Writing a word <strong>sound by sound</strong>, with one symbol for each sound, is <strong>phonetic transcription</strong>. Dictionaries use the symbols of the <strong>International Phonetic Alphabet (IPA)</strong>. A symbol is put between slashes, /k/, or square brackets, [k], to show that it is a <strong>sound</strong>, not a letter.</p>\n<p>Example from the lecture’s table: <em>thing</em> has 5 letters but 3 sounds: /θɪŋ/.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The slide <strong>English Letters</strong> gives the name of each letter and the sounds in that name, in square brackets (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xcc0U_U4xhg&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>). Even one letter name usually has <strong>two or more sounds</strong>: <em>bee</em> is [biː], a /b/ and a long /iː/. The names fall into patterns:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Pattern of the name</th>\n<th>Letters (name in sounds)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>consonant + long /iː/</td>\n<td>B [biː], C [siː], D [diː], G [dʒiː], P [piː], T [tiː], V [viː], Z [ziː] (American)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>consonant + /eɪ/</td>\n<td>J [dʒeɪ], K [keɪ]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>vowel + consonant</td>\n<td>F [ɛf], L [ɛl], M [ɛm], N [ɛn], S [ɛs], X [ɛks]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>one vowel sound only</td>\n<td>A [eɪ], E [iː], I [aɪ], O [oʊ]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the rest</td>\n<td>H [(h)eɪtʃ], Q [kjuː], R [ɑː] or [ɑr], U [juː], W [ˈdʌbəljuː], Y [waɪ], Z [zɛd] (British)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em>Double-u</em> [ˈdʌbəljuː] “is like a word” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xcc0U_U4xhg&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>). (The mark ˈ shows the stressed, stronger part.)</p>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">The same sound, two spellings in the slides</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>This letter chart writes the vowel of <em>pet</em> as ɛ and the vowel of <em>toe</em> as oʊ (the American way). The vowel chart below writes them e and əʊ (the British way). The slides also sometimes write i for ɪ, u for ʊ and a colon : for ː. The sounds are the same; only the symbols differ.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Five vowel letters, twenty vowel sounds</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Ask “how many vowels does English have?” and people often say five: a, e, i, o, u. That is not wrong for <strong>writing</strong>: these five letters represent vowel sounds. But English has <strong>20 vowel sounds</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xcc0U_U4xhg&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-44-sounds-of-english\">The 44 sounds of English</h3>\n<p>All speech sounds fall into two classes: <strong>consonants</strong> and <strong>vowels</strong>. Vowels are “more fundamental to natural language” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xcc0U_U4xhg&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>); Lecture 1.7 shows why: no word is possible without one.</p>\n<p><strong>The 20 vowel sounds.</strong> The slide <strong>Vowels</strong> gives each sound with a word in which it is underlined (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xcc0U_U4xhg&amp;t=1410s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:30</a>); the third column adds the key word for the same sound from the chart in Lecture 1.6. The ː mark means <strong>long</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sound</th>\n<th>Word (1.3)</th>\n<th>Word (1.6 chart)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>/ɪ/</td>\n<td>pit</td>\n<td>sit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/e/</td>\n<td>pet</td>\n<td>men</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/æ/</td>\n<td>pat</td>\n<td>cat</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/ɒ/</td>\n<td>pot</td>\n<td>not</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/ʌ/</td>\n<td>but</td>\n<td>but</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/ʊ/</td>\n<td>book</td>\n<td>book</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/ə/</td>\n<td>mother</td>\n<td>America</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/iː/</td>\n<td>bean</td>\n<td>read</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/ɜː/</td>\n<td>burn</td>\n<td>word</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/ɑː/</td>\n<td>barn</td>\n<td>part</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/ɔː/</td>\n<td>born</td>\n<td>sort</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/uː/</td>\n<td>boon</td>\n<td>too</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/aɪ/</td>\n<td>bite</td>\n<td>my</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/eɪ/</td>\n<td>bait</td>\n<td>day</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/ɔɪ/</td>\n<td>boy</td>\n<td>boy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/əʊ/</td>\n<td>toe</td>\n<td>go</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/aʊ/</td>\n<td>house</td>\n<td>how</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/ʊə/</td>\n<td>poor</td>\n<td>tour</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/ɪə/</td>\n<td>ear</td>\n<td>here</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/eə/</td>\n<td>air</td>\n<td>wear</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The first 12 are <strong>monophthongs</strong> (one vowel quality); the last 8 are <strong>diphthongs</strong> (a glide). Lecture 1.6 explains the difference.</p>\n<p><strong>The 24 consonant sounds.</strong> The slide <strong>Consonant Sounds in words</strong> numbers them 1 to 24 with one word each (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xcc0U_U4xhg&amp;t=1680s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:00</a>); a second table at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xcc0U_U4xhg&amp;t=1890s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:30</a> and the chart in Lecture 1.6 give more words.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sound</th>\n<th>Word (1.3 list)</th>\n<th>More words (1.3 table, 1.6 chart)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>/p/</td>\n<td>pit</td>\n<td>pin, pig</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/b/</td>\n<td>bit</td>\n<td>bad, bed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/t/</td>\n<td>tab</td>\n<td>tin, time</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/d/</td>\n<td>dab</td>\n<td>dog, do</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/k/</td>\n<td>cab</td>\n<td>kind, kilo</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/g/</td>\n<td>gab</td>\n<td>gun, go</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/f/</td>\n<td>fan</td>\n<td>five</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/v/</td>\n<td>van</td>\n<td>very</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/s/</td>\n<td>sad</td>\n<td>say, six</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/z/</td>\n<td>zoo</td>\n<td>zoo</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/m/</td>\n<td>man</td>\n<td>milk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/n/</td>\n<td>not</td>\n<td>nose, no</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/h/</td>\n<td>hot</td>\n<td>half, hello</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/l/</td>\n<td>lad</td>\n<td>leg, live</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/r/</td>\n<td>red</td>\n<td>run, read</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/w/</td>\n<td>wed</td>\n<td>work, window</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/θ/</td>\n<td>thought</td>\n<td>thin, think</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/ð/</td>\n<td>them</td>\n<td>that, the</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/ʃ/</td>\n<td>shy</td>\n<td>ship, short</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/ʒ/</td>\n<td>treasure</td>\n<td>measure, casual</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/tʃ/</td>\n<td>chime</td>\n<td>church</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/dʒ/</td>\n<td>jam</td>\n<td>judge</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/j/</td>\n<td>yum</td>\n<td>yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/ŋ/</td>\n<td>sing</td>\n<td>thing</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Symbols that look like letters</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>/j/ is the <strong>y sound</strong> of <em>yes</em> and <em>yum</em>. The j of <em>jam</em> is /dʒ/.</li>\n<li>The spelling th stands for <strong>two</strong> sounds: /θ/ in <em>thin, thought, think</em> and /ð/ in <em>that, them, the</em>.</li>\n<li>/ʃ/ is the sh of <em>shy</em> and <em>ship</em>; /ʒ/ is the sound in the middle of <em>treasure, measure, casual</em>.</li>\n<li>/ŋ/ is <strong>one</strong> sound: the ng of <em>sing</em> and <em>thing</em>.</li>\n<li>/ə/ is the weak vowel at the end of <em>mother</em> and at both ends of <em>America</em>.</li>\n<li>/tʃ/ and /dʒ/ are written with two symbols but count as <strong>one</strong> sound each.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>Some words from the lecture’s tables, written sound by sound (with the symbols of the vowel chart):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Word</th>\n<th>Sounds</th>\n<th>Word</th>\n<th>Sounds</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>pin</td>\n<td>/pɪn/</td>\n<td>thing</td>\n<td>/θɪŋ/</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>bad</td>\n<td>/bæd/</td>\n<td>half</td>\n<td>/hɑːf/</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>five</td>\n<td>/faɪv/</td>\n<td>church</td>\n<td>/tʃɜːtʃ/</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>thin</td>\n<td>/θɪn/</td>\n<td>jam</td>\n<td>/dʒæm/</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>that</td>\n<td>/ðæt/</td>\n<td>ship</td>\n<td>/ʃɪp/</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>man</td>\n<td>/mæn/</td>\n<td>measure</td>\n<td>/meʒə/</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>nose</td>\n<td>/nəʊz/</td>\n<td>yes</td>\n<td>/jes/</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The lecture’s exercise: find <strong>five words for each vowel sound</strong> (a list of 100 words), and five for each consonant. Do it slowly, one set at a time (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xcc0U_U4xhg&amp;t=1560s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">44 sounds, infinite words</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>English has only <strong>44 sounds</strong> (24 consonants + 20 vowels), yet they make an <strong>infinite</strong> number of words: nobody knows the total number of words in any language. That a finite set of sounds gives an infinite set of words is “no less than a miracle” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xcc0U_U4xhg&amp;t=1830s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:30</a>). For writing, these 44 sounds are represented with only <strong>26 letters</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xcc0U_U4xhg&amp;t=1980s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 33:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"how-speech-sounds-are-made\">How speech sounds are made</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 1.4. Instead of “practising things blindly”, learn <strong>where</strong> and <strong>how</strong> each sound is made. Then you can compare an English sound with the nearest sound of your own language and change only what is different (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MMBrfQC0V9A&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>). Sounding better adds to confidence.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"vowels-and-consonants\">Vowels and consonants</h3>\n<p>We breathe air in to the lungs and breathe it out. Speech uses the <strong>exhaling</strong> flow of air (the air going out), not the inhaling flow. On its way out, this air is <strong>modified</strong> at different places in the vocal tract, and that gives the different sounds (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MMBrfQC0V9A&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Vowel and consonant</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Vowel</strong>: a sound made with <strong>minimal obstruction</strong> of the exhaling air (<em>obstruction</em> = something in the way). People say vowels come from “free” air; that is a slight exaggeration, because every sound needs some modification.</li>\n<li><strong>Consonant</strong>: a sound made by modifying the exhaling air “to a great extent in multiple ways”: blocking it or narrowing its way.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>(<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MMBrfQC0V9A&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">The hold test</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Say <em>aaa</em> or <em>eee</em>: you can hold a vowel for as long as your breath lasts. Now try to hold the consonant k: what you can stretch is the vowel after it (<em>kaaa</em>), not the k itself (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MMBrfQC0V9A&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>In normal speech we keep vowels short. But <strong>length</strong> matters: it can be the only difference between two words. The slide <strong>Articulation of Vowel Sounds</strong> shows six fundamental vowels in <strong>three short-long pairs</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MMBrfQC0V9A&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>). They exist in most languages of the world, including English (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MMBrfQC0V9A&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>). The IPA column uses the symbols the 20-vowel chart gives to the same sounds.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Slide label</th>\n<th>Length</th>\n<th>IPA</th>\n<th>Lecture’s words</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>a</td>\n<td>short</td>\n<td>/ʌ/</td>\n<td>up, cup</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>aa</td>\n<td>long</td>\n<td>/ɑː/</td>\n<td>father, sound, round</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>i</td>\n<td>short</td>\n<td>/ɪ/</td>\n<td>in, ink, sink, drink</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ii</td>\n<td>long</td>\n<td>/iː/</td>\n<td>clean, seat, beat, feet</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>u</td>\n<td>short</td>\n<td>/ʊ/</td>\n<td>book, cook, look</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>uu</td>\n<td>long</td>\n<td>/uː/</td>\n<td>zoo, boot, room</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>(In <em>sound</em> and <em>round</em> the vowel is really the diphthong /aʊ/ of Lecture 1.6, which starts from this open a.)</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">seat and sit (the lecture’s pair at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MMBrfQC0V9A&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><em>seat</em> has three sounds: /s/, /iː/, /t/.</li>\n<li><em>sit</em> has three sounds: /s/, /ɪ/, /t/.</li>\n<li>The consonants are the same. Only the vowel differs: long /iː/ in <em>seat</em>, short /ɪ/ in <em>sit</em>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> vowel length alone makes them two words with two meanings.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-articulators\">The articulators</h3>\n<p>The slide <strong>Articulators</strong> is a side view of the head. Its labels:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>lips</strong> and <strong>teeth</strong>;</li>\n<li><strong>alveolar ridge</strong>: the hard bump just behind the upper front teeth, where the gum holds them (the lecture calls it “the muscular area where we have our upper teeth fixed”);</li>\n<li><strong>palate</strong>: the hard roof of the mouth;</li>\n<li><strong>velum</strong>: the soft back part of the roof of the mouth;</li>\n<li><strong>uvula</strong>: the small piece of flesh that hangs at the back of the mouth (you see it when you open your mouth wide);</li>\n<li><strong>tongue tip</strong>, <strong>tongue blade</strong> (just behind the tip) and <strong>tongue back</strong>;</li>\n<li><strong>oral cavity</strong> (the mouth), <strong>nasal cavity</strong> (the space inside the nose), <strong>pharyngeal cavity</strong> (the throat);</li>\n<li><strong>epiglottis</strong>; <strong>larynx</strong> (voice box) with the <strong>vocal folds</strong>; <strong>glottis</strong> (the opening between the vocal folds).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Velum, palate, alveolar ridge, teeth and lips are <strong>fixed places</strong>. The one organ that moves back and forward to touch them is the <strong>tongue</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MMBrfQC0V9A&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Place of articulation</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The <strong>place of articulation</strong> of a consonant is the point in the vocal tract where the exhaling air is blocked or narrowed. The lecture names five in our languages (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MMBrfQC0V9A&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Velar</strong>: the back of the tongue touches the <strong>velum</strong> (<em>ka</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Palatal</strong>: the body (blade) of the tongue touches the <strong>palate</strong>; the tip plays no role (<em>cha</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Retroflex</strong>: the <strong>tip</strong> of the tongue <strong>curls (folds) backward</strong> and strikes the alveolar region (<em>retroflex</em> = bent back).</li>\n<li><strong>Dental</strong>: the tip of the tongue at the <strong>teeth</strong> (<em>ta</em>, as in our languages).</li>\n<li><strong>Labial</strong> or <strong>bilabial</strong>: both <strong>lips</strong> come together (<em>pa</em>).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>The slide <strong>Articulation of Consonant Sounds</strong> arranges 25 consonants of our languages by these places (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MMBrfQC0V9A&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>). The columns use two features that Lecture 1.5 explains: asp = aspiration (a puff of air), voice = voicing (vibration in the throat). On the slide the first four columns are −asp −voice, +asp −voice, −asp +voice and +asp +voice; here each cell holds two of those sounds, the −asp one first, so that the chart fits on a phone.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Place</th>\n<th>−voice: −asp, +asp</th>\n<th>+voice: −asp, +asp</th>\n<th>Nasal</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Velar</td>\n<td>k, kh</td>\n<td>g, gh</td>\n<td>ng</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Palatal</td>\n<td>c, ch</td>\n<td>j, jh</td>\n<td>ny</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Retroflex</td>\n<td>T, Th</td>\n<td>D, Dh</td>\n<td>N</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dental</td>\n<td>t, th</td>\n<td>d, dh</td>\n<td>n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Labial</td>\n<td>p, ph</td>\n<td>b, bh</td>\n<td>m</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>In this chart the capitals T, D, N are retroflex sounds and the small t, d, n are dental. The palatal j here is the <em>ja</em> sound of our languages, <strong>not</strong> the IPA symbol /j/. The 20 sounds of the slide’s first four columns (the two middle columns here) are <strong>oral</strong>; the 5 sounds of the last column are <strong>nasal</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MMBrfQC0V9A&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Oral and nasal sounds</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Say <em>pa</em>, then <em>ma</em>; then <em>ta</em>, then <em>na</em>, and notice the difference (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MMBrfQC0V9A&amp;t=1530s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:30</a>). It comes from the <strong>uvula</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MMBrfQC0V9A&amp;t=1620s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Uvula raised</strong>: it blocks the way into the nasal cavity, so all the air goes out through the mouth. The sound is <strong>oral</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Uvula lowered</strong>: part of the air also goes out through the nasal cavity. The sound is <strong>nasal</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>So <em>pa</em> is an oral bilabial sound and <em>ma</em> is a nasal bilabial sound: same place, different path for the air (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MMBrfQC0V9A&amp;t=1710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Uvula and velum</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Many books say the <strong>velum</strong> (soft palate) rises and falls here. The uvula hangs from the end of the velum, so the two move together. The lecture uses the word uvula.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-english-consonant-chart\">The English consonant chart</h3>\n<p>The last chart places the English consonants by <strong>place</strong> (columns) and <strong>manner</strong> (rows) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MMBrfQC0V9A&amp;t=1800s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:00</a>). Read by place:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Place</th>\n<th>How it is made</th>\n<th>English sounds</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Bilabial</td>\n<td>both lips</td>\n<td>/p/, /b/, /m/</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Labiodental</td>\n<td>lower lip and upper teeth</td>\n<td>/f/, /v/</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dental</td>\n<td>tongue tip at the teeth</td>\n<td>/θ/, /ð/</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alveolar</td>\n<td>tongue tip at the alveolar ridge</td>\n<td>/t/, /d/, /n/, /s/, /z/, /l/, and the r sound (ɾ, ɹ)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Post-alveolar</td>\n<td>just behind the alveolar ridge</td>\n<td>/ʃ/, /ʒ/</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Palatal</td>\n<td>tongue body at the palate</td>\n<td>/j/</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Velar</td>\n<td>tongue back at the velum</td>\n<td>/k/, /g/, /ŋ/</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Glottal</td>\n<td>at the glottis</td>\n<td>/h/, /ʔ/</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em>Labiodental</em> = lip (labio) + teeth (dental); <em>post-alveolar</em> = after (post) the alveolar ridge. The lecture reads the columns (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MMBrfQC0V9A&amp;t=1830s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:30</a>): three bilabial sounds, two labiodental, two dental, the alveolar sounds, <em>sha</em> and <em>zha</em> post-alveolar, one palatal sound, three velar sounds, and two glottal sounds.</p>\n<p>The rows give the <strong>manner of articulation</strong>: how the air is stopped or let through. The lecture comes back to manner in Lectures 1.5 and 1.7. In short:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>stop</strong>: the air is fully blocked, then let go (/p/, /b/, /t/, /d/, /k/, /g/, /ʔ/);</li>\n<li><strong>nasal</strong>: the mouth is blocked and the air goes out through the nose (/m/, /n/, /ŋ/);</li>\n<li><strong>flap</strong>: one quick tap of the tongue tip (ɾ);</li>\n<li><strong>fricative</strong>: the air is pushed through a narrow gap and makes a hissing, rubbing noise (/f/, /v/, /θ/, /ð/, /s/, /z/, /ʃ/, /ʒ/, /h/);</li>\n<li><strong>approximant</strong>: two parts come close but not close enough to hiss (ɹ, /j/);</li>\n<li><strong>lateral approximant</strong>: the air flows round the sides (<em>lateral</em> = side) of the tongue (/l/).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">What this chart shows and leaves out</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>The two glottal sounds are /h/ and <strong>/ʔ/</strong>, the glottal stop: the short catch in the throat in the middle of <em>uh-oh</em>.</li>\n<li>The r sound appears twice: ɾ (flap) and ɹ (approximant). They are two ways of saying the one English /r/.</li>\n<li>/w/, /tʃ/ and /dʒ/ of the 24-sound list are not in this chart. /w/ uses both lips with the back of the tongue raised; /tʃ/ and /dʒ/ are made just behind the alveolar ridge, like /ʃ/ and /ʒ/.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>Many of these English sounds are not part of our sound systems, so we need to learn them separately and make small changes in the way we speak (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MMBrfQC0V9A&amp;t=1890s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:30</a>). If we bring in the sounds of our own languages, we will sound the way we usually sound.</p>\n<h2 id=\"aspiration-voicing-and-indian-english\">Aspiration, voicing and Indian English</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 1.5. Place alone does not tell sounds apart: <em>ka, kha, ga</em> and <em>gha</em> all come from the velum. Two more features, <strong>aspiration</strong> and <strong>voicing</strong>, give each sound its own identity. With them we can see exactly which English sounds differ from ours.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"describing-vowels\">Describing vowels</h3>\n<p>The slide <strong>Understanding vowel sounds</strong> places the vowels on a chart: “English vowels. The figure represents the mouth” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fC-UbX3MsGs&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>). Imagine the chart inside your mouth, with the lips on the left.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Front, central, back; close, mid, open</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Front, central, back</strong>: which part of the tongue is raised: the front, the middle or the back.</li>\n<li><strong>Close, mid, open</strong>: <strong>tongue height</strong>. Close = the tongue is high, close to the roof of the mouth. Open = the tongue is low and the mouth is open.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>Roughly, from the slide:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Front</th>\n<th>Central</th>\n<th>Back</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Close</td>\n<td>/iː/ (bean), /ɪ/ (pit)</td>\n<td></td>\n<td>/uː/ (boon), /ʊ/ (book)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mid</td>\n<td>/e/ (pet)</td>\n<td>/ɜː/ (burn), /ə/ (mother)</td>\n<td>/ɔː/ (born)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Open</td>\n<td>/æ/ (pat)</td>\n<td>/ʌ/ (but)</td>\n<td>/ɑː/ (barn), /ɒ/ (pot)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h3 id=\"aspiration-and-voicing\">Aspiration and voicing</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Aspiration</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A small <strong>extra puff of air</strong> that comes out with a sound. Test: hold your palm in front of your mouth and say <em>ka</em>, then <em>kha</em>. Only with <em>kha</em> do you feel a small blow on your palm (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fC-UbX3MsGs&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>). No puff is written <strong>−asp</strong> (minus aspiration); a puff is <strong>+asp</strong>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Voicing</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Inside the larynx are the <strong>vocal folds</strong> with the <strong>vocal cords</strong>. For some sounds the cords <strong>vibrate</strong> more than usual; this vibration is <strong>voicing</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fC-UbX3MsGs&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>). Test: put your palm on the front of your throat and say <em>ka</em>, then <em>ga</em>. With <em>ga</em> you feel a buzz (resonance). No vibration is <strong>−voice</strong> (a <strong>voiceless</strong> sound); vibration is <strong>+voice</strong> (a <strong>voiced</strong> sound).</p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Velar sound</th>\n<th>Aspiration</th>\n<th>Voicing</th>\n<th>What you feel</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>k</td>\n<td>−asp</td>\n<td>−voice</td>\n<td>no puff, no buzz</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>kh</td>\n<td>+asp</td>\n<td>−voice</td>\n<td>a puff, no buzz</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>g</td>\n<td>−asp</td>\n<td>+voice</td>\n<td>no puff, a buzz</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>gh</td>\n<td>+asp</td>\n<td>+voice</td>\n<td>a puff and a buzz</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Place + aspiration + voicing gives every one of the 20 oral sounds its own identity (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fC-UbX3MsGs&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Naming a sound from its features (the lecture’s method at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fC-UbX3MsGs&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>The lecture’s case: from the lips, no aspiration, no voicing. In the labial row, the −asp −voice column holds only <strong>p</strong>.</li>\n<li>A second case (mine): from the lips, aspiration <strong>and</strong> voicing. The labial row, +asp +voice column, holds <strong>bh</strong>.</li>\n<li>A third case (mine): from the velum, through the nose. The velar row, nasal column, holds <strong>ng</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> p, bh and ng. One place plus two features points to exactly one cell of the chart.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Two palm tests</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Palm <strong>in front of the mouth</strong> → aspiration (a puff). Palm <strong>on the throat</strong> → voicing (a buzz). There is nothing embarrassing about doing these tests yourself; do them once for every sound.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"english-has-no-retroflex-sounds\">English has no retroflex sounds</h3>\n<p>The whole <strong>retroflex row</strong> of the chart (T, Th, D, Dh, N) is <strong>not</strong> in English (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fC-UbX3MsGs&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>). In the English chart, /p/ and /b/ are both bilabial stops, /p/ voiceless and /b/ voiced, and /m/ is nasal (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fC-UbX3MsGs&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>). The sounds to watch are /t/ and /d/: in English they are <strong>alveolar</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fC-UbX3MsGs&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">English t and d</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The <strong>tip of the tongue</strong>, without curling back, <strong>gently touches the alveolar ridge</strong>. It is “a straight, simple alveolar sound”, not retroflex.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Our languages are full of retroflex sounds, so we tend to carry them into English (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fC-UbX3MsGs&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Dravidian</strong> languages (Tamil, Telugu, Malayalam, Kannada) are full of retroflex sounds.</li>\n<li><strong>Indo-Aryan</strong> languages (Hindi, Punjabi, Bhojpuri, Magahi, Odia, Bangla and many more) also have many, though not as many as the Dravidian languages.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>So everybody in India is “in the same bracket”. This is one feature that gives our English its <strong>Indianness</strong>.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Indian English</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“The kind of English spoken in different parts of India is named as <strong>Indian English</strong>, just as English spoken in America is called American English, or English spoken in Britain is called British English” (also Australian English and African English) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fC-UbX3MsGs&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>). It is a <strong>variety</strong> of English, and “there is nothing bad or offensive about it”.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Why it happens: the vocal tract <strong>matures before 12 years of age</strong> according to the sound patterns of our native languages, and after that it does not change. “There is scientific foundation for how and why anyone speaks the way they do” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fC-UbX3MsGs&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>). Still, with regular practice we can take in special sounds of another language: use the non-retroflex, alveolar t and d when you speak English, and keep the retroflex ones for Tamil, Hindi or Punjabi (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fC-UbX3MsGs&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">ph is not f</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecturer underlines /f/ and /v/ in the English chart as two sounds rarely found in our languages, and takes /f/ as the example (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fC-UbX3MsGs&amp;t=1410s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>ph</strong> of Hindi (as in <em>phal</em>, <em>phool</em>): aspirated, voiceless, <strong>bilabial</strong>: both lips.</li>\n<li><strong>f</strong> of English (as in <em>father, French, fat, friend</em>): <strong>labiodental</strong>: the upper teeth touch the lower lip.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Saying <em>phather</em> for <em>father</em> replaces an English sound with one of ours.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Do the maths</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Put the 24 consonants and 20 vowels of English next to the sounds of your own language. Many are common to both. What remains is a <strong>small set</strong> of sounds; work on that set and you can sound the way you want in English (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fC-UbX3MsGs&amp;t=1590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"diphthongs\">Diphthongs</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 1.6. English has 44 sounds: 20 vowels and 24 consonants. Looking closer, some of the 20 vowels are <strong>diphthongs</strong>, and some of the consonants are <strong>semi-vowels</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WqiKRiJTqnc&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>). Knowing these types of sounds, and the places and manners of articulation, helps pronunciation “immensely”.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Monophthong and diphthong</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Monophthong</strong>: a vowel with <strong>one vowel quality</strong>: it starts and ends the same (/ɪ/ in <em>sit</em>). English has <strong>12</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Diphthong</strong>: a vowel that <strong>starts as one vowel and ends as another</strong>: the tongue <strong>glides</strong> from one position to another within one vowel (/eɪ/ in <em>take</em>). English has <strong>8</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WqiKRiJTqnc&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The slide’s definition (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WqiKRiJTqnc&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>): “Diphthongs are sounds which consist of a movement or a glide from one vowel to the other. In other words, diphthongs are produced by combining two vowels. E.g. /ei/ is a diphthong that combines the two vowels /e/ and /i/. The first sound in the combination is articulated longer and stronger than the second one.” In length, diphthongs are like long vowels.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">The names</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>Mono</em> = one, <em>di</em> = two; <em>-phthong</em> comes from the Greek word for sound. So 12 one-quality vowels + 8 two-quality vowels = 20.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The eight diphthongs, with the slide’s word (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WqiKRiJTqnc&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>) and the words of the <strong>Examples</strong> table (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WqiKRiJTqnc&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Diphthong</th>\n<th>Glides from → to</th>\n<th>As in</th>\n<th>More words</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>/eɪ/</td>\n<td>e → ɪ</td>\n<td>take</td>\n<td>aim, prey, break, late</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/aɪ/</td>\n<td>a → ɪ</td>\n<td>buy</td>\n<td>high, ice, fly, type, right</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/ɔɪ/</td>\n<td>ɔ → ɪ</td>\n<td>boy</td>\n<td>oil, alloy, voice, toy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/ɪə/</td>\n<td>ɪ → ə</td>\n<td>fear</td>\n<td>peers, ear, clear, real</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/eə/</td>\n<td>e → ə</td>\n<td>care</td>\n<td>pair, air, bear, their</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/əʊ/</td>\n<td>ə → ʊ</td>\n<td>go</td>\n<td>slow, owe, float, lone</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/ʊə/</td>\n<td>ʊ → ə</td>\n<td>poor</td>\n<td>jury, cure, fuel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/aʊ/</td>\n<td>a → ʊ</td>\n<td>cow</td>\n<td>owl, brow, allow</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The Examples table writes the headings with plain letters (ei, ai, oi, əu, au, iə, uə, eə); they are the same eight. It also lists <em>bowl</em> under /aʊ/, but <em>bowl</em> has the /əʊ/ of <em>go</em>.</p>\n<p>The lecturer writes some of these words sound by sound (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WqiKRiJTqnc&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>): <em>take</em> /teɪk/, <em>buy</em> /baɪ/, <em>boy</em> /bɔɪ/, <em>fear</em> /fɪə/, <em>care</em> /keə/. <em>Take</em> has only <strong>three</strong> sounds: consonant, vowel, consonant. Its t is also “a little aspirated”, [tʰ]; the course returns to aspiration in English in <a href=\"#/english1/w08\">Week 8</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">A diphthong is one sound</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>On paper /aɪ/ looks like two vowels. But when we speak, the glide is a <strong>single</strong> vowel sound, not two (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WqiKRiJTqnc&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>). So <em>buy</em> /baɪ/ has two sounds, and <em>take</em> /teɪk/ has three.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Exercise from the lecture: transcribe these words and more, find the eight diphthongs in other words (you can take help online), and practise the difference between monophthongs and diphthongs.</p>\n<h2 id=\"semi-vowels\">Semi-vowels</h2>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Semi-vowel</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WqiKRiJTqnc&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>“The sounds <strong>w</strong> and <strong>y</strong> in English are called semi-vowels.” (In IPA: /w/ and /j/.)</li>\n<li>“There is no blockage of air in the articulation of w and y, thus giving it a vowel-like quality.”</li>\n<li>“However, w and y are <strong>non-syllabic</strong> in nature. This means that unlike vowels they do not form syllables.”</li>\n<li>“Owing to the above reason, they are referred to as <strong>consonants</strong>.”</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>The consonant chart of Lecture 1.6 ends with /r/, /w/ and /j/. The lecturer groups these last three together and names /w/ and /j/ in particular as the semi-vowels (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WqiKRiJTqnc&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>).</p>\n<p>Why the name (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WqiKRiJTqnc&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>)?</p>\n<ul>\n<li>They are consonants, but they <strong>function like vowels</strong>: they help vowels. So: <em>semi-vowels</em>.</li>\n<li>Why not <em>semi-consonants</em>? Because they are <strong>full consonants</strong>.</li>\n<li>Why not full vowels? Because they are <strong>not syllabic</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Syllable and syllabic</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>syllable</strong> is a smaller unit of a word. Some words are one syllable (<em>monosyllabic</em>); others have more. <strong>Every syllable must have a vowel sound</strong>, so vowels are called <strong>syllabic</strong> sounds (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WqiKRiJTqnc&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>). A semi-vowel alone cannot make a syllable: the syllable still needs a vowel. <a href=\"#/english1/w08\">Week 8</a> returns to syllables.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Consonants are always made with some obstruction in the vocal tract. A semi-vowel is a consonant made <strong>without, or with very little, obstruction</strong>, like a vowel (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WqiKRiJTqnc&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"sound-not-spelling\">Sound, not spelling</h3>\n<p>The <strong>Examples</strong> slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WqiKRiJTqnc&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>/w/</th>\n<th>/j/ (the y sound)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>well</td>\n<td>yesterday</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>world</td>\n<td>intrusion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>twin</td>\n<td>beauty</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>suite</td>\n<td>steward</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>acquire</td>\n<td>few</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The sound is not always spelled with w or y (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WqiKRiJTqnc&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>/w/ <strong>with</strong> the letter w: <em>well, world, twin</em> (in the middle of the word).</li>\n<li>/w/ <strong>without</strong> a w: <em>suite</em> (the u), <em>acquire</em> and <em>question</em> (the u after q gives /kw/).</li>\n<li>/j/ <strong>without</strong> a y: <em>beauty, steward, few</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>(The slide also lists <em>intrusion</em>; most dictionaries today give it as /ɪnˈtruːʒn/, without /j/, so learn <em>beauty, steward, few</em> as the safe examples.)</p>\n<h3 id=\"never-at-the-end-of-a-word\">Never at the end of a word</h3>\n<p>Semi-vowels <strong>do not occur at the word-final position</strong> (the end of a word) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WqiKRiJTqnc&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>). Someone may object with <em>window, cow, happy, Monday</em>. But we are talking about <strong>sounds</strong>, not spelling:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Word</th>\n<th>Ends in the letter</th>\n<th>Ends in the sound</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>window</td>\n<td>w</td>\n<td>/əʊ/, a diphthong</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>cow</td>\n<td>w</td>\n<td>/aʊ/, a diphthong</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>happy</td>\n<td>y</td>\n<td>/i/, a vowel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Monday</td>\n<td>y</td>\n<td>/eɪ/, a diphthong</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>None of them ends in a semi-vowel (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WqiKRiJTqnc&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"their-function-joining-two-vowels\">Their function: joining two vowels</h3>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>When one word ends with a vowel sound and the next word begins with a vowel sound, the voice must pass from one vowel to the other. In normal, fast speech a semi-vowel <strong>appears between them</strong> to help this transition (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WqiKRiJTqnc&amp;t=1530s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:30</a>). This is “one of the most important functions” of semi-vowels, and it happens in other languages too.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The slide <strong>Functions of Semi-vowels</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WqiKRiJTqnc&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Phrase</th>\n<th>First word ends in</th>\n<th>Semi-vowel heard</th>\n<th>Sounds like</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Go away</td>\n<td>/əʊ/</td>\n<td>/w/</td>\n<td>go-<strong>w</strong>-away</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I am late.</td>\n<td>/aɪ/</td>\n<td>/j/</td>\n<td>I-<strong>y</strong>-am late</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Say it.</td>\n<td>/eɪ/</td>\n<td>/j/</td>\n<td>say-<strong>y</strong>-it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Buy it.</td>\n<td>/aɪ/</td>\n<td>/j/</td>\n<td>buy-<strong>y</strong>-it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Put your shoe on</td>\n<td>/uː/</td>\n<td>/w/</td>\n<td>shoe-<strong>w</strong>-on (“shoe won”)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Blue is my favourite colour.</td>\n<td>/uː/</td>\n<td>/w/</td>\n<td>blue-<strong>w</strong>-is</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Three years</td>\n<td>/iː/</td>\n<td>/j/</td>\n<td>three-<strong>y</strong>-ears</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Which semi-vowel?</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The choice is not random; it is fully patterned (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WqiKRiJTqnc&amp;t=1650s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>after a <strong>rounded</strong> vowel (lips pushed into a circle, as at the end of <em>go, shoe, blue</em>) → <strong>/w/</strong>;</li>\n<li>otherwise (as at the end of <em>I, say, buy, three</em>, where the lips are spread) → <strong>/j/</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Two lines of that slide</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><em>Blue is</em>: the handwritten mark on the slide is /y/, but <em>blue</em> ends in the rounded vowel /uː/, so by the lecturer’s own rule the linking sound is /w/, as in <em>shoe on</em>.</li>\n<li><em>Three years</em>: here the second word already begins with /j/, so the linking y and the first sound of <em>years</em> are the same sound.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>Notice this when you listen to people who grew up speaking English. Then their speech will be easier to follow (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WqiKRiJTqnc&amp;t=1710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:30</a>).</p>\n<h2 id=\"consonant-clusters\">Consonant clusters</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 1.7. A word has only two kinds of sounds: consonants (C) and vowels (V). A <strong>vowel is mandatory</strong>: no word can be made without one, in any language. Most words <strong>alternate</strong> consonants and vowels. A <strong>cluster</strong> is where consonants come together with no vowel between them (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Pj1iWVqBuyg&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"words-as-patterns-of-c-and-v\">Words as patterns of C and V</h3>\n<p>The slide <strong>Simple words</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Pj1iWVqBuyg&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>) shows the patterns, with its own simple spellings of the sounds:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Pattern</th>\n<th>Words</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>CVCV</td>\n<td>papa, daddy /daedii/, mommy /momii/</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CVC</td>\n<td>dad, mom</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>VC</td>\n<td>am, is, are, it, in, on, at</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CVV</td>\n<td>the /dii/, sea /sii/</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>vowels only</td>\n<td>eye /aai/</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Papa</em>: pa (C), a (V), pa (C), a (V): four sounds.</li>\n<li><em>Daddy</em>: d, a, d, ii: four sounds. The slide’s <em>ae</em> stands for <strong>one</strong> vowel sound, not two (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Pj1iWVqBuyg&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>).</li>\n<li><em>Dad</em> and <em>mom</em>: three sounds each, CVC (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Pj1iWVqBuyg&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>).</li>\n<li>Here the lecturer writes a long vowel (the ii of <em>sea</em>) and the vowel of <em>eye</em> as two V’s.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Papa and daddy, dad and mom mean the same thing; pay attention to the <strong>sounds</strong>, not the spelling. A word without a consonant is possible but rare: <em>eye</em> has only vowel sounds (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Pj1iWVqBuyg&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>). The same is true in every language: check your own.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-a-cluster-is\">What a cluster is</h3>\n<p>Say any consonant alone: g, p, j. You hear <em>ga, pa, ja</em>. Every consonant has a <strong>built-in short vowel a</strong>; we cannot say a consonant alone without it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Pj1iWVqBuyg&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Consonant cluster</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slides (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Pj1iWVqBuyg&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>“Two consonant sounds together without the inbuilt sound ‘a’ from the first one.”</li>\n<li>“Reduction of the inbuilt vowel sound from the first one.”</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Only <strong>consonants</strong> form clusters, never vowels: “it is the absence of the vowel that is called a cluster” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Pj1iWVqBuyg&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">glass and sink (the lecture’s examples at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Pj1iWVqBuyg&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><em>glass</em> has four sounds: g, l, aa, s. Its pattern is <strong>CCVC</strong>.</li>\n<li>We say <em>glass</em>, not <em>galass</em>: the built-in a after g is gone, so <strong>g and l form a cluster</strong> [gl].</li>\n<li><em>sink</em> has four sounds: s, i, n, k. Its pattern is <strong>CVCC</strong>.</li>\n<li>n and k come together with no vowel between them: <strong>[nk] is a cluster</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>glass</em> has a cluster at the start (gl); <em>sink</em> has one at the end (nk).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The two sounds of a cluster come from <strong>different places</strong>: g is velar and l is a lateral liquid (<em>liquid</em> is the lecture’s name for /l/ and /r/); n is a nasal sound and k is velar (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Pj1iWVqBuyg&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"manner-and-the-same-place-rule\">Manner and the same-place rule</h3>\n<p>Two sounds from the same place are told apart by their <strong>manner of articulation</strong>, the way they are made (aspiration, voicing). The slide <strong>Consonants</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Pj1iWVqBuyg&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sound</th>\n<th>Place</th>\n<th>Features</th>\n<th>Words</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>/k/</td>\n<td>velar</td>\n<td>−asp, −voice</td>\n<td>king, class</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/g/</td>\n<td>velar</td>\n<td>−asp, +voice</td>\n<td>great, glass</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/p/</td>\n<td>bilabial</td>\n<td>−asp, −voice</td>\n<td>pink, pure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>/b/</td>\n<td>bilabial</td>\n<td>−asp, +voice</td>\n<td>bliss, bless</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>“They have an inbuilt vowel sound ‘a’ with all of them.” In these words: <em>king</em> has no cluster at the start (k is followed by a vowel); <em>class</em> has kl, <em>great</em> gr, <em>glass</em> gl, <em>bliss</em> and <em>bless</em> bl; <em>pink</em> has none at the start but nk at the end (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Pj1iWVqBuyg&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">p is voiceless, b is voiced</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In the video the lecturer once says it the other way round. The slide, and the palm test of Lecture 1.5, are right: /p/ is −voice and /b/ is +voice. The same holds for /k/ (−voice) and /g/ (+voice).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The same-place rule</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“No two sounds from the same place of articulation (other than nasals) will become part of the cluster” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Pj1iWVqBuyg&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>pb</em>, <em>bp</em> (both bilabial) and <em>td</em> (both from the same place) do not form clusters.</li>\n<li><em>mb</em> is fine: one nasal and one non-nasal sound <strong>can</strong> cluster even from the same place (as in <em>number</em>, my example).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>In careful IPA the n of <em>sink</em> is /ŋ/, the velar nasal, so <em>sink</em> is /sɪŋk/. Then /ŋk/ is a nasal and a non-nasal from the same place, which is exactly the kind of cluster the rule allows.</p>\n<h3 id=\"clusters-anywhere-but-not-in-random-order\">Clusters anywhere, but not in random order</h3>\n<p>A cluster can come at the <strong>beginning</strong>, in the <strong>middle</strong> or at the <strong>end</strong> of a word, and one word can have <strong>two</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Pj1iWVqBuyg&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>). The slide’s examples, with its simple spellings:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Word</th>\n<th>Slide’s spelling</th>\n<th>Pattern</th>\n<th>Clusters</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>cluster</td>\n<td>/kləstər/</td>\n<td>CCVCCVC</td>\n<td>kl (start), st (middle)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>bliss</td>\n<td>/blis/</td>\n<td>CCVC</td>\n<td>bl</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>school</td>\n<td>/skuul/</td>\n<td>CCVC</td>\n<td>sk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>scooter</td>\n<td>/skutar/</td>\n<td>CCVCVC</td>\n<td>sk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>blast</td>\n<td>/blaast/</td>\n<td>CCVCC</td>\n<td>bl (start), st (end)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Why clusters exist: with only 44 sounds, English has millions of words. There is no fixed limit on how many sounds or syllables a word has. Words of four, five, six or seven sounds are normal, and to have more sounds in a word we need clusters. So clusters are natural in every language (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Pj1iWVqBuyg&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"clusters-of-three-consonants\">Clusters of three consonants</h3>\n<p>A cluster of <strong>three</strong> consonants is possible too, especially at the <strong>beginning</strong> of a word (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Pj1iWVqBuyg&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a>). The slide <strong>Clusters of three consonants</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Word</th>\n<th>Slide’s spelling</th>\n<th>Cluster</th>\n<th>Pattern</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>spring</td>\n<td>/spring/</td>\n<td>s p r</td>\n<td>CCCVC</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>stress</td>\n<td>/stres/</td>\n<td>s t r</td>\n<td>CCCVC</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>screw</td>\n<td>/skrue/</td>\n<td>s k r</td>\n<td>CCCVV</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>splash</td>\n<td>/splash/</td>\n<td>s p l</td>\n<td>CCCVC</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>string</td>\n<td>/string/</td>\n<td>s t r</td>\n<td>CCCVC</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>scrub</td>\n<td>/skrub/</td>\n<td>s k r</td>\n<td>CCCVC</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The three-consonant template</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Look down the columns (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Pj1iWVqBuyg&amp;t=1620s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>first sound: always <strong>s</strong>, a fricative;</li>\n<li>second sound: <strong>p, t or k</strong>, all voiceless stops;</li>\n<li>third sound: <strong>r or l</strong>, the liquids.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>s + {p, t, k} + {r, l}.</strong> “No word with a cluster of three consonant sounds in the beginning gets any sound other than these” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Pj1iWVqBuyg&amp;t=1770s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>More words to test it, from the slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Pj1iWVqBuyg&amp;t=1710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:30</a>): <em>scream, scroll, scrutiny, stress, scrap, strand, strict, strip, spring</em>. Every one starts with s, then p, t or k, then r or l.</p>\n<p>(In this lecture /t/ is called a “dental stop”; in the English chart of Lecture 1.4, English /t/ is alveolar.)</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Limits on clusters</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>No</strong> English word begins with a cluster of <strong>four</strong> consonants (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Pj1iWVqBuyg&amp;t=1830s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:30</a>).</li>\n<li>At the <strong>end</strong> of a word, four consonants are possible, but in very few words (for example <em>texts</em>: k, s, t, s; my example).</li>\n<li>Words beginning with three consonants are also few.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Look for patterns</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Languages are rule governed and they have definite structure in it. As students of data science, you need to keep your eyes open for finding patterns” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Pj1iWVqBuyg&amp;t=1800s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:00</a>). Take 20 words and see how many have clusters (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Pj1iWVqBuyg&amp;t=1410s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:30</a>). Noticing clusters improves both vocabulary and pronunciation.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"summary\">Summary</h2>\n<section class=\"box sm\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">In short</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Learning</strong> (1.1, 1.2): accuracy → fluency → confidence → communicative confidence. Acquisition is automatic (children, before about 10); learning needs effort (after about 15). Input (fuzzy, limited) → the mind (LAD/UG) → output (grammatical, infinite). Grown-ups: observe patterns, apply them, let the subconscious work. The first language conditions the vocal tract; sound like yourself. Listen 30 minutes and read 30 minutes a day.</p>\n<p><strong>Letters and sounds</strong> (1.3): 26 letters, 44 sounds (20 vowels + 24 consonants); no one-to-one correspondence (“A for apple” teaches the letter, not the sound). A sound is written in IPA between slashes: /k/.</p>\n<p><strong>How sounds are made</strong> (1.4): the exhaling air. Vowel = minimal obstruction (can be held); consonant = the air is blocked or narrowed. Places: bilabial, labiodental, dental, alveolar, post-alveolar, palatal, velar, glottal (and retroflex in our languages). Uvula raised → oral; lowered → nasal. Vowel length can change the word (<em>seat</em>, <em>sit</em>).</p>\n<p><strong>Features</strong> (1.5): aspiration = a puff (palm in front of the mouth); voicing = vibration of the vocal cords (palm on the throat). k (−asp −voice), kh (+asp −voice), g (−asp +voice), gh (+asp +voice). Vowels: front/central/back, close/mid/open. English has no retroflex: /t/ and /d/ are alveolar; /f/ is labiodental, not ph. Indian English is a variety.</p>\n<p><strong>Diphthongs</strong> (1.6): 12 monophthongs + 8 diphthongs = 20 vowels. The 8: /eɪ/ take, /aɪ/ buy, /ɔɪ/ boy, /ɪə/ fear, /eə/ care, /əʊ/ go, /ʊə/ poor, /aʊ/ cow. One glide = one sound; the first part is longer and stronger.</p>\n<p><strong>Semi-vowels</strong> (1.6): /w/ and /j/: consonants with no blockage, but non-syllabic. Never at the end of a word. They join a vowel to a vowel across words: /w/ after a rounded vowel (go-w-away), /j/ otherwise (I-y-am).</p>\n<p><strong>Clusters</strong> (1.7): every word needs a vowel. Cluster = consonants together, with the built-in a of the first one gone (glass, sink). Not two non-nasals from the same place (<em>pb</em>, but <em>mb</em> is fine). Anywhere in a word, even twice (cluster). Three at the start: s + p/t/k + r/l (spring, stress, splash, string, scrub, screw). Never four at the start.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"check-yourself\">Check yourself</h2>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Acquisition or learning? (a) A three-year-old starts saying words in Tamil at home. (b) A 20-year-old engineer studies Japanese in an evening class, with a job in Japan in mind.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li>(a) <strong>Acquisition.</strong> It happens automatically, before 10 years of age, from the input around the child; nobody teaches it.</li>\n<li>(b) <strong>Learning.</strong> It needs conscious effort after 15, and it is helped by a <em>specific and directed motivation</em> (the job). Another language already exists, and the vocal tract is already conditioned by it.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">How many letters and how many sounds are in <em>thing</em> and in <em>church</em>? Write each in IPA.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li><em>thing</em>: 5 letters (t, h, i, n, g). Sounds: /θ/ (th), /ɪ/, /ŋ/ (ng). So 3 sounds: /θɪŋ/.</li>\n<li><em>church</em>: 6 letters (c, h, u, r, c, h). Sounds, as in the lecture’s table (where the r is silent): /tʃ/, /ɜː/, /tʃ/. So 3 sounds: /tʃɜːtʃ/.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 5 letters but 3 sounds, and 6 letters but 3 sounds. Letters and sounds do not match one to one.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">/p/, /b/ and /m/ are all bilabial. What tells them apart?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li>/p/: oral (uvula raised), <strong>−voice</strong>: no buzz on the throat.</li>\n<li>/b/: oral, <strong>+voice</strong>: the vocal cords vibrate.</li>\n<li>/m/: <strong>nasal</strong>: the uvula is lowered and part of the air goes out through the nose.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The place is the same (both lips); the <strong>voicing</strong> separates /p/ and /b/, and the <strong>path of the air</strong> (oral or nasal) separates /m/ from both.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Monophthong or diphthong? The vowel of <em>cup</em>, <em>cow</em>, <em>book</em>, <em>boy</em>, <em>seat</em>, <em>care</em>.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li><em>cup</em>: /ʌ/, <strong>monophthong</strong> (one quality).</li>\n<li><em>cow</em>: /aʊ/, <strong>diphthong</strong> (glides from a to ʊ).</li>\n<li><em>book</em>: /ʊ/, <strong>monophthong</strong>.</li>\n<li><em>boy</em>: /ɔɪ/, <strong>diphthong</strong>.</li>\n<li><em>seat</em>: /iː/, <strong>monophthong</strong> (long, but still one quality).</li>\n<li><em>care</em>: /eə/, <strong>diphthong</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Length is not the test: /iː/ is long but is a monophthong. Ask whether the vowel <strong>changes</strong> while you say it.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which semi-vowel joins the words in <em>two eggs</em>, <em>my aunt</em>, <em>no answer</em> and <em>see it</em>? And does <em>window</em> end in a semi-vowel? (The phrases are mine.)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li><em>two eggs</em>: <em>two</em> ends in /uː/, a rounded vowel → <strong>/w/</strong> (two-w-eggs).</li>\n<li><em>my aunt</em>: <em>my</em> ends in /aɪ/, not rounded → <strong>/j/</strong> (my-y-aunt).</li>\n<li><em>no answer</em>: <em>no</em> ends in /əʊ/, which ends rounded → <strong>/w/</strong> (no-w-answer).</li>\n<li><em>see it</em>: <em>see</em> ends in /iː/, lips spread → <strong>/j/</strong> (see-y-it).</li>\n<li><em>window</em> ends in the <strong>letter</strong> w but in the <strong>sound</strong> /əʊ/, a diphthong. Semi-vowels never come at the end of a word.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Find the clusters and the CV pattern of <em>strict</em>. Then: can an English word begin with <em>spl</em>, <em>sbr</em> or <em>ptr</em>?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li><em>strict</em> = s, t, r, i, k, t: <strong>CCCVCC</strong>. Clusters: <strong>str</strong> at the start and <strong>kt</strong> at the end.</li>\n<li><em>spl</em>: <strong>yes</strong>. It fits s + p + l, as in <em>splash</em>.</li>\n<li><em>sbr</em>: <strong>no</strong>. The second sound must be p, t or k, voiceless stops; b is voiced.</li>\n<li><em>ptr</em>: <strong>no</strong>. A three-consonant cluster at the start must begin with s.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> only <em>spl</em> is possible. Check new words against the template s + {p, t, k} + {r, l}.</p>\n</div></details>\n"});
